     "pages": 11,
     "head": "Dance as Visual Storytelling10 Activity 3.1 Beyond words Observe Fig. 3.1. It is a picture of a stage set before the performance begins. As a member of the audience, look at what you notice and what you understand from it. Answer the questions given below based on what you observe. Remember that these questions do not have ‘one correct answer’. Each of you can give a different answer based on your perception and understanding. Let your creativity flow! Questions 1. What do you think is implied by the two levels created on stage? 2. What is the first object that caught your attention? What do you think it represents? Fig. 3.1. Stage set before performance begins Śhṛiṅgāra Rasa What are the other forms of communicating to the audience? Why are they necessary? While we often think that only actors speak and communicate with the audience, it is interesting to note that everything put up on s"
    },
    {
     "file": "iemr104",
     "pages": 10,
     "head": "Creativity in Light and Sound4 Imagine you are just waking up and you open your eyes to a fantasy world made entirely of light. Soft lavender clouds glow beneath your feet, pathways shimmer in patterns of turquoise and gold, and towering trees made of sparkling beams sway gently as if alive. Neon green fireflies zip past, leaving trails like strokes of paint in the air . You look above, to see the sky with ripples of rainbow colours. In this magical place, light doesn’t just show the world. Light creates it. And amazingly, this is not far from reality. With today’s stage lighting technology, designers can shape colours, moods, worlds, and emotions just like this, turning imagination into a living experience on stage. Lighting: How It All Started From the time of early cave dwellers to modern Broadway shows, we humans have always told stories. The human mind loves telling and listening to"
    },
    {
     "file": "iemr105",
